WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
After a script validates an argument, it still has to pass that value safely.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.280
Validation checks whether a value is acceptable.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.180
Quoting preserves the value when Bash expands it.

4
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.040
These are different controls.

5
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.980
A validated path can still be split into multiple words if it is expanded without quotes.

6
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.480
A validated label can still be treated as a filename pattern if globbing is allowed to reinterpret it.

7
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.600
Quoting tells Bash to keep a value together as one argument.

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.940
This matters for paths, labels, environment names, filenames, and option values.

9
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.360
When forwarding the original argument list, "$@" preserves each argument boundary.

10
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:47.100
That lets a wrapper function or helper script receive the same arguments the caller supplied, rather than a re-split approximation.

11
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:55.220
Arrays are especially useful in Bash because they let scripts build argument lists without turning them into one large command string.

12
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:04.920
A script can append a flag, an option value, or a path as separate array elements and then pass the array to a command with preserved boundaries.

13
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.460
This is easier to review than constructing a string that the shell later interprets.

14
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.620
Defensive Bash scripts keep data as data.

15
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.680
They avoid evaluating constructed command strings from uncontrolled or loosely controlled values.

16
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.460
They prefer passing separate arguments to the command being called.

17
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:32.160
The safety habit is consistent: validate first, quote expansions, use arrays for argument lists, and keep shell syntax separate from command-line data.
